Sum of XOR of all pairs

https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/sum-of-xor-of-all-pairs0723/1

Difficulty: MediumAccuracy: 45.14%Submissions: 59K+Points: 4

Given an integer array arr[] of size n, compute the sum of the bitwise XOR for all distinct pairs of elements. 
That is, consider every pair of indices (i, j) such that 0 ≤ i < j < n, and calculate the total sum of (arr[i] XOR arr[j]) over all such pairs.

Examples :

Input : arr[] = [7, 3, 5]
Output : 12
Explanation: 
All pairs (i, j) such that i < j and their XOR values are:
7 ^ 3 = 4
7 ^ 5 = 2
3 ^ 5 = 6
Sum of all XOR values = 4 + 2 + 6 = 12.

Input : arr[] = [5, 9, 7, 6] 
Output : 47
Explanation:
All pairs (i, j) such that i < j and their XOR values are:
5 ^ 9 = 12
5 ^ 7 = 2
5 ^ 6 = 3
9 ^ 7 = 14
9 ^ 6 = 15
7 ^ 6 = 1
Sum of all XOR values = 12 + 2 + 3 + 14 + 15 + 1 = 47.

Input : arr[] = [10] 
Output : 0
Explanation: Since there are no pairs, sum is 0.
Constraints
1 ≤ n ≤ 10^5
1 ≤ arr[i] ≤ 10^5

APPROACH 1: BRUTE FORCE APPROACH: using nested loop ans finding every possible pair and its xor values.

APPROACH 2: OPTIMAL APPROACH: here using the bit manipulation concept xor give result as 1 only when it is 0,1 -->1, 1,0 -->1 ;
                              so here basically we are counting set and unset bits and then finding the possible combinations that is just the product of 
                              set and unset bit and atlast to get the sum we are multiplying the possible combinations to 2^power of i jonse bit position pe ho.

CODE : 

class Solution {
public:
    long long sumXOR(vector<int> &arr) {
        // code here
        long long ans = 0;

        int n = arr.size();

        // Brute force (commented)
        /*
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int xo = 0;
            for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                xo = arr[i] ^ arr[j];
                ans += xo;
            }
        }
        */

        for(int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
            long long setbit = 0, unsetbit = 0;

            for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if((arr[j] & (1 << i)) != 0)   // check ith bit
                    setbit++;
                else
                    unsetbit++;
            }

            long long possible_combinations = setbit * unsetbit;
            ans += possible_combinations * (1LL << i);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
